{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"A\":\"Build a general serial algorithmic candidate. Replace per-column quadratic bubble sort and quadratic mode counting with a reference-compatible O(rows log rows) stable sort (reuse scratch storage) plus linear equal-run mode detection. Preserve source ordering: mean/harmonic before sorting, variance over sorted values using the original mean, exact equality/tie behavior, and output formatting. Handle signed zero, NaN/token edge cases only if they match the reference.\",\"B\":\"Build a coarse-grained 4-core candidate: parallelize independent whole-column work, retain deterministic per-column arithmetic, and use a dimension/work-based serial fallback so thread startup cannot lose on the 9.315 ms baseline. Measure complete process time at 1/2/4 workers; do not assume parallel reductions or combining changes is safe.\",\"C\":\"Build a distinct low-level serial candidate. Investigate buffered standards-compatible input parsing, fused independent scans, removal of repeated allocations, and safe numeric-kernel improvements; retain arithmetic order where rounding can affect %.1f output. Compare library/custom stable sorting approaches, but accept only variants preserving the source-observed mode and comparison semantics on broad inputs.\",\"G\":\"Integrate only validated candidates. Differential-check broad valid matrices (small/large, odd/even rows, duplicates/ties, zero/negative/extreme doubles and input formatting), then measure baseline, each candidate, and only compatible combinations repeatedly under the unchanged environment. The measured baseline is already very short and integration may regress; retain the fastest correct end-to-end program, including baseline if necessary.\",\"analysis\":\"The valid baseline averages 9.315 ms on a 4-core ARM64 target, so fixed I/O and thread costs matter. The clear general bottlenecks in the source are quadratic sorting and mode calculation; parsing and pow/scans are secondary. Preserve exact observable behavior rather than exploiting one-decimal output or test-specific values, especially the source's sorted-mode/tie and floating-point evaluation behavior.\"}"}}
